From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000086 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gemini Mainline Experiment <gemini-mainline@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 19 Jul 2026 16:10:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: mediatek: use MT8173 data for MT6797

The MT6797 Device Tree already carries the SoC-specific compatible before
its MT6577 fallback, but the driver has no matching entry.  It therefore
uses the MT6577 data, which disables automatic repeated starts and the
separate auxiliary receive-length register.

The exact working Gemini 3.18 binary converts a register-address write plus
read into hardware WRRD.  It writes the receive length at offset 0x6c.
Those properties match the existing MT8173 data.  An independent Gemini
hardware report also recovered combined reads by selecting that data for
MT6797.

Match MT6797 directly to the MT8173 controller data.  This is a narrow
local integration step; a named MT6797 data structure can replace it if
upstream review identifies a distinct controller contract.
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-mt65xx.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-mt65xx.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-mt65xx.c
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-mt65xx.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-mt65xx.c
@@ -527,6 +527,7 @@ static const struct of_device_id mtk_i2c_of_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt2712-i2c", .data = &mt2712_compat },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt6577-i2c", .data = &mt6577_compat },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt6589-i2c", .data = &mt6589_compat },
+	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-i2c", .data = &mt8173_compat },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt7622-i2c", .data = &mt7622_compat },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt7981-i2c", .data = &mt7981_compat },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt7986-i2c", .data = &mt7986_compat },
